Once I got used to the vi-style keys, I've found them to be much
preferable to the number pad way of moving around.   Some people like
it, some don't.

If you'd like to give it a try, use the 'O' command in-game to set the
"number_pad" option to "0" (false).  If you end up liking the way it
works, put this line in your defaults.nh:

OPTIONS=number_pad:0

The basic idea starts with the keys 'h','j','k', and 'l', which are all
in a row on the keyboard.  'h' is on the far left, and is used to move
left.  'l' is on the right, and moves right.  'j' moves down, and 'k'
moves up.  For diagonal movement, 'y' is up-left, 'u' is up-right, 'b'
is down-left, and 'n' is down-right.

It was pretty confusing to me at first, but in just a couple of days of
playing I was totally used to it and find it much, much easier on the
wrists than continually moving back and forth from the number pad and
the regular keys.

As far as conflicts with other commands, there are other ways of doing
all of them.  'k', for example, does the same thing as ^d (<ctrl>+d).
The other alternatives are all listed in the online help or in the
Guidebook.

Not using the number_pad doesn't work for everybody, and that's fine.
Choose whatever solution you like.  But there are plenty of us who
really dig it, especially when playing on laptops.

